<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <title>Text Adventure</title>
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">
    <%= csrf_meta_tags %>
    <%= render "application/icons", theme: "#14161a" %>
    <%# THE LOADING SCREEN -- the blinking block cursor alone, centred on the
        page's own background, and nothing else: no new colour, no image to
        fetch. Nothing paints until the head has arrived, and the page's
        stylesheet is most of a page's bytes, so the head carries only these
        few rules and the stylesheet follows the screen into the body: on a
        slow link the cursor shows while the rest is still on its way, where
        before there was a blank page for as long as the stylesheet took.
        `#ready` -- the last thing in the body -- hides it once the whole
        page has been parsed. `:has()` is the switch, so a browser without it
        never shows it at all rather than showing it for ever.

        The same screen covers a Turbo visit or form post that is still
        waiting on the server (Play, Resume, a link): Turbo marks the document
        `aria-busy` for as long as one is in flight. There it fades in only
        after a moment, so a quick page never flashes it. %>
    <style>
      html, body { background: #14161a; }
      .loading { display: none; }
      @supports selector(:has(*)) {
        body:not(:has(> #ready)) > .loading, html[aria-busy="true"] .loading {
          display: flex; position: fixed; inset: 0; z-index: 2;
          align-items: center; justify-content: center; background: #14161a;
          color: #8ba0b4; font: 24px ui-monospace, "DejaVu Sans Mono", monospace;
        }
        html[aria-busy="true"] .loading { animation: loading-in .2s .4s both; }
      }
      @keyframes loading-in { from { opacity: 0; } }
      .loading::after { content: "\2588"; animation: blink 1s steps(2) infinite; }
      @keyframes blink { 50% { opacity: 0; } }
    </style>
    <%# Where the page posts its own uncaught errors; absent unless
        `ERRORS_COMMAND` is set (config/initializers/pocket_kit.rb). %>
    <%= pocket_kit_errors_tag %>
    <%= javascript_importmap_tags %>
  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="loading" role="status" aria-label="Loading"></div>
    <%# Still one inline <style>: propshaft is here to serve JavaScript, and the
        reading experience is a stage of its own (`ta-api-iface`). Restyling
        anything is that task's job, not this file's. It sits in the body,
        after the loading screen, so that screen can paint before it arrives.
        pocket-kit's feedback stylesheet comes first so the block can colour it. %>
    <%= stylesheet_link_tag "pocket_kit/feedback" %>
    <style>
      :root { color-scheme: dark; }
      body {
        background: #14161a; color: #cfd3d8;
        font: 15px/1.6 ui-monospace, "DejaVu Sans Mono", monospace;
        margin: 0; padding: 2rem 1rem;
      }
      /* NO SIDEWAYS SCROLL ON A PHONE. A token too long for the column -- a
         run-on name, a hyphenated room, anything a model writes without a
         space -- breaks inside itself rather than pushing the page wider;
         `overflow-wrap` inherits, so this one line covers the log, the place
         line and every panel. The body is never `overflow-x: hidden`: that
         would hide content instead of fitting it. */
      main { max-width: 44rem; margin: 0 auto; overflow-wrap: break-word; }
      h1 { font-size: 1.2rem; letter-spacing: .08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: #8ba0b4; }
      h2 { font-size: 1rem; color: #e6e9ec; margin: 0 0 .25rem; }
      a { color: #7fb2d8; }
      hr { border: 0; border-top: 1px solid #262b31; margin: 1.5rem 0; }
      .preface { color: #9aa3ac; font-style: italic; white-space: pre-wrap; }
      .turn { white-space: pre-wrap; margin: 0 0 1.5rem; }

      /* The log dims every turn but the newest, so a player who has scrolled
         can find where the turn they just took begins. Past turns take the
         preface's colour; the newest keeps full-strength body colour. Colour
         only -- the preface is italic as well, and a turn is not.
         `:last-of-type` is the marker: no class per entry, and on a brand new
         playthrough the opening scene is the only turn and so the newest one.
         While a turn is streaming the newest turn is the #stream div *below*
         the log, so the log carries `streaming` and nothing in it brightens.
         `.entry` is the wrapper one turn's prose, verdict and machinery panel
         share (`turns/_turn`), and it is what `:last-of-type` counts now: it
         used to be `.log > .turn:last-of-type`, which quietly depended on
         nothing under a turn ever being a `<div>`. Four tests assert this
         selector directly. */
      .log .turn { color: #9aa3ac; }
      .log:not(.streaming) > .entry:last-of-type .turn { color: #cfd3d8; }
      .command { color: #d8c07f; margin: 0 0 .35rem; }
      .speaking { color: #8b939b; margin: 0 0 .35rem; }
      .place { color: #8b939b; margin: 0 0 .75rem; }
      .place strong { color: #cfd3d8; font-weight: normal; }
      .story { border: 1px solid #262b31; padding: .75rem 1rem; margin-bottom: .75rem; }
      .story p { color: #8b939b; margin: .25rem 0 .75rem; }
      form { display: flex; gap: .5rem; }
      input[type=text], textarea {
        flex: 1; background: #0e1013; color: #cfd3d8; border: 1px solid #333b44;
        padding: .5rem .6rem; font: inherit; min-width: 0;
      }
      /* iOS zooms the page in when a field under 16px takes focus, and the
         zoomed page then scrolls sideways -- on the play page that is every
         turn, since the input is autofocused. 16px on a touch screen only, so
         the desktop look keeps the body's 15px. */
      @media (pointer: coarse) {
        input[type=text], textarea { font-size: max(16px, 1em); }
      }
      /* A GAME THE PLAYER NARRATES: the mode choice beside Play, and the
         paragraph box on the facts card. Layout only, out of what is here. */
      .play-mode { flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; }
      .play-mode label { color: #8b939b; }
      .paragraph-box { flex-direction: column; align-items: flex-start; margin: .5rem 0 0; }
      .paragraph-box textarea { width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; resize: vertical; }
      /* `form.submit` renders an input, `button_to` a button; one house style. */
      button, input[type=submit] {
        background: #262b31; color: #cfd3d8; border: 1px solid #3a424b;
        padding: .5rem .9rem; font: inherit; cursor: pointer;
      }
      button:hover, input[type=submit]:hover { background: #333b44; }
      .cursor::after { content: "\2588"; animation: blink 1s steps(2) infinite; }
      @keyframes blink { 50% { opacity: 0; } }
      .empty { color: #6b737b; }
      .alert { color: #d88f7f; border: 1px solid #4a3230; padding: .5rem .75rem; }

      /* The app talking rather than the world -- see Playthrough::SafetyNotice.
         Deliberately not `.alert`: nothing has gone wrong and the alarm colour
         would say it had. A left rule and the preface's grey put it plainly
         outside the fiction without shouting, and `.turn`'s pre-wrap is absent
         on purpose because this text is the app's own paragraphs rather than a
         model's. It sits at the foot of the log, where the turn would have
         been. */
      .notice {
        border-left: 2px solid #4a5a4a; padding: .1rem 0 .1rem .75rem;
        margin: 0 0 1.5rem; color: #9aa3ac;
      }
      .notice p { margin: .5rem 0; }
      .notice strong { color: #cfd3d8; font-weight: normal; }

      /* THE SLASH MENU -- what the play box can complete this turn with. It is
         a list under the input rather than an overlay: the input sits at the
         foot of the page, the log above it is what the player is reading, and a
         floating panel would cover the turn they just took. `.ask` is the
         positioning context and the only thing between the form and the page.

         Styled out of the pieces already here -- the input's own border and
         background, the log's dim grey for the hint -- so the menu reads as
         part of the box and not as a widget bolted to it. */
      .ask { position: relative; }
      .slash-menu {
        list-style: none; margin: .35rem 0 0; padding: 0;
        border: 1px solid #333b44; background: #0e1013; max-height: 12rem; overflow-y: auto;
      }
      .slash-option {
        padding: .25rem .6rem; cursor: pointer;
        display: flex; gap: .75rem; align-items: baseline; justify-content: space-between;
      }
      .slash-option[aria-selected="true"] { background: #262b31; color: #e6e9ec; }
      .slash-hint { color: #6b737b; font-size: 12px; }

      /* A SHEET: THE ENGINE'S OWN RECORDS, RENDERED RATHER THAN NARRATED.

         `.sheet` is the frame, `.battle` is the one thing built on it today
         (`playthroughs/_battle`). Split that way on the d3 rooms scout's call
         5, which asks that the battle panel and a future ROOM SHEET share a
         partial and a stylesheet: the room sheet is not built here and the
         captain has not ruled on it, but nothing in these five rules is about
         a fight, so it can be had without a restyle.

         Built out of what is already in this file -- `.story`'s border, `h1`'s
         uppercase heading, the log's dim grey for anything secondary -- so a
         panel reads as part of the page and not as a widget bolted to it. NO
         BARS AND NO NEW COLOURS: the condition is `2 of 6` in the notice grey,
         because the reading experience is `ta-api-iface`'s stage and inventing
         a visual vocabulary in a battle panel is that stage arriving early
         (`data/ta-combat-scout` §15.6). */
      .sheet { border: 1px solid #262b31; padding: .6rem .85rem; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
      .sheet h2 {
        font-size: .8rem; letter-spacing: .08em; text-transform: uppercase;
        color: #8ba0b4; margin: 0 0 .5rem;
      }
      .sheet-line {
        display: flex; gap: .75rem; align-items: baseline; justify-content: space-between;
        margin: .1rem 0;
      }
      .sheet-line .who { color: #cfd3d8; }
      .sheet-line .state { color: #9aa3ac; margin-left: auto; }
      .sheet-line .mark { color: #6b737b; font-size: 12px; min-width: 5rem; text-align: right; }
      .sheet-note { color: #8b939b; margin: .5rem 0 0; }
      /* WHAT JUST HAPPENED, AND WHAT YOU DO NOW -- the two ends of a round, and
         the panel is read between them. `.sheet-lead` is the engine's line
         about the round that is DONE, in the body colour because it is the
         first thing to read; `.sheet-prompt` names the round the buttons under
         it land on, in the heading's own small-caps so a row of buttons cannot
         read as a mode that was entered. No new colours: both are already in
         this file. */
      .sheet-lead { color: #cfd3d8; margin: 0 0 .6rem; }
      .sheet-prompt {
        font-size: .8rem; letter-spacing: .08em; text-transform: uppercase;
        color: #8ba0b4; margin: .9rem 0 0;
      }
      /* `form` is flex and full-width in the rule above, which would put every
         button on a line of its own. The same fix `.verdict` makes. */
      .sheet-actions { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .4rem; margin: .7rem 0 0; }
      .sheet-actions form { display: inline-flex; margin: 0; }
      .sheet-actions input[type=submit] { font-size: 13px; padding: .3rem .7rem; }

      /* The one colour a fight adds, and it is the alert colour already here:
         which of the bodies on the panel means the party harm. */
      .battle .mark.hostile, .battle .mark.provoked { color: #d88f7f; }

      /* THE MACHINERY BEHIND ONE TURN, and it is the play page's own vocabulary
         rather than the debug page's.

         The captain's ask of 2026-09-05: *"I want to be able to select any given
         turn and see the current game state as well as the exact prompt that was
         given to the narrator ... I can't compare the two side by side."* So on
         a wide viewport the panel sits in the right-hand gutter beside the turn
         it belongs to, and on a narrow one it stacks under it. The debug page
         has its own layout and stylesheet BY DESIGN -- it is allowed to be as
         dense as it likes precisely because the two share no CSS -- so nothing
         here is imported from it; these are the play page's colours, `.sheet`'s
         frame and the log's dim grey, spelled out again at a smaller size.

         The control is barely visible until the entry is hovered, for the same
         reason the verdict is: there is one under every turn and the log is what
         the player is reading. An open panel stays lit. */
      .machinery { margin: -1rem 0 1.5rem; }
      .machinery > summary {
        color: #4f565d; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; padding: .1rem 0;
        transition: color .12s;
      }
      .entry:hover .machinery > summary, .machinery > summary:focus { color: #8b939b; }
      .machinery[open] > summary { color: #cfd3d8; }

      /* The panel itself, and `.machinery-panel` rather than `.machinery` is the
         scope on purpose: with scripts blocked the same partial is served as a
         page of its own and there is no `<details>` around it there. */
      .machinery-panel { font-size: 13px; }
      .machinery-panel .sheet { margin: .4rem 0 .6rem; }
      /* A LABEL COLUMN AND A VALUE COLUMN, and the provenance mark rides with
         the value rather than in a column of its own. `.sheet-line` is flex with
         the mark pushed right, which is correct across the width of the page and
         wrong in a panel a third of it: at the narrow end of the breakpoint
         below the mark wrapped onto a line by itself and the five rows stopped
         reading as five rows. Same three spans, same vocabulary, laid out for
         the width it actually gets. */
      .machinery-panel .sheet-line {
        display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 9rem) minmax(0, 1fr);
        gap: 0 .8rem; margin: .15rem 0;
      }
      .machinery-panel .sheet-line .who { color: #6b737b; }
      .machinery-panel .sheet-line .state { margin-left: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
      .machinery-panel .sheet-line .mark {
        min-width: 0; text-align: left; margin-left: .35rem; white-space: nowrap;
      }
      .machinery-panel .sheet-line .mark::before { content: "\00b7\00a0"; }
      .machinery-panel .absent { color: #7a6a52; font-style: italic; }
      .machinery-panel .note { color: #6b737b; }
      .machinery-panel h3 {
        font-size: 11px; letter-spacing: .06em; text-transform: uppercase;
        color: #6b737b; font-weight: normal; margin: .7rem 0 .1rem;
      }
      /* One stored conversation, collapsed -- `debug/_conversation`, rendered
         here so the play page and the debug page cannot come to disagree about
         what was sent. A turn has three or four of these and an arrival prompt
         inlines the whole universe, so open the one you want. */
      .machinery-panel .exchange { margin: 0 0 .2rem; }
      .machinery-panel .exchange > summary { color: #8b939b; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; }
      .machinery-panel .exchange > summary:hover { color: #cfd3d8; }
      .machinery-panel .body {
        padding: .3rem 0 .6rem .7rem; border-left: 1px solid #21262c; margin-left: .25rem;
      }
      .machinery-panel .tag { color: #8b939b; border: 1px solid #33393f; padding: 0 .3rem; font-size: 11px; }
      .machinery-panel .tag.purpose { color: #7fb2d8; border-color: #2f4a5c; }
      .machinery-panel .tag.durable { color: #7fd8c0; border-color: #2f5a50; }
      .machinery-panel .tag.version { color: #b79ad8; border-color: #4a3d5c; }
      /* Wide content scrolls inside its own box; the page body never does. */
      .machinery-panel pre {
        background: #0e1013; border: 1px solid #21262c; color: #9aa3ac;
        padding: .4rem .5rem; margin: .2rem 0 0; font: inherit; font-size: 12px;
        max-height: 24rem; overflow: auto; white-space: pre-wrap; overflow-wrap: anywhere;
      }

      /* SIDE BY SIDE, WHEN THERE IS ROOM FOR IT -- and stacked when there is
         not, which is the whole of the responsive story here.

         The reading column stays exactly where it is: `main` is 44rem and
         centred, and the panel is pushed into the right-hand gutter with a
         negative margin on the entry, so the prose does not move a pixel when a
         panel opens. `--panel` is what fits in that gutter, capped at 34rem, and
         the breakpoint is where it stops being worth having: at 88rem of
         viewport the gutter gives 20rem. Below that everything falls back to the
         block layout above and the panel stacks under the turn.

         `:has()` is the guard, and it is load-bearing rather than decorative: an
         entry with no panel (the flag is off) must not be widened, or the prose
         would reflow to the width of a column that is not there. A browser
         without `:has()` drops the whole block and gets the stacked layout,
         which is the correct fallback. */
      @media (min-width: 88rem) {
        .log .entry:has(> .machinery) {
          --panel: min(34rem, calc((100vw - 44rem) / 2 - 2rem));
          display: flex; gap: 1rem; align-items: flex-start;
          margin-right: calc(-1 * (var(--panel) + 1rem));
        }
        .log .entry > .said { flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; max-width: 44rem; }
        .log .entry > .machinery { flex: 0 0 var(--panel); margin-top: 0; }
      }

      /* THE VERDICT ON A TURN -- the evaluation instrument, and the least
         styling that makes it usable without competing with the prose. The
         reading experience is still `ta-api-iface`'s job; this is not the
         restyling, it is one control kept out of the way of it.

         Barely visible until the row is hovered, because there is one of these
         under every turn in the log and the log is what the player is reading.
         The chosen verdict stays lit whether or not anything is hovered -- it
         is a record, not a hover state -- and each verdict keeps its own colour
         so a scrolled log can be scanned for the turns that were marked.

         `form` is flex and full-width in the rule above, which would put each
         of these on a line of its own; inline-flex is what keeps the whole
         control on one. */
      .verdict {
        display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .4rem; align-items: center;
        margin: -1rem 0 1.5rem; opacity: .28; transition: opacity .12s;
      }
      .verdict:hover, .verdict:focus-within { opacity: 1; }
      .verdict:has(.chosen) { opacity: .85; }
      .verdict form { display: inline-flex; gap: .3rem; margin: 0; }
      .verdict input[type=submit], .verdict button {
        background: none; border: 1px solid #2b3238; color: #6b737b;
        padding: .05rem .4rem; font: inherit; font-size: 12px; cursor: pointer;
      }
      .verdict input[type=submit]:hover, .verdict button:hover { background: #1c2126; color: #cfd3d8; }
      .verdict .mark.chosen { border-color: currentColor; }
      .verdict .mark.good.chosen { color: #7fd8c0; }
      .verdict .mark.weak.chosen { color: #d8c07f; }
      .verdict .mark.bad.chosen { color: #d88f7f; }
      /* The note is a single line and sits beside the buttons rather than
         stretching to the width of the log. */
      .verdict .why input[type=text] {
        flex: 0 1 18rem; padding: .05rem .4rem; font-size: 12px; border-color: #2b3238;
      }

      /* A NOTE TO THE MAINTAINER -- pocket-kit's feedback button, configured in
         config/initializers/pocket_kit.rb. Its stylesheet is linked above this
         block, so these rules win: one muted control at the top right of the
         page, in the verdict buttons' quiet style, coloured through the gem's
         custom properties from this palette. "Sent" is the notice grey; a
         failure inside the dialog is the `.alert` colour. */
      .pk-feedback {
        --pk-feedback-color: #cfd3d8; --pk-feedback-background: #14161a; --pk-feedback-muted: #6b737b;
        --pk-feedback-border: #2b3238; --pk-feedback-radius: 0; --pk-feedback-backdrop: rgba(0, 0, 0, .5);
        --pk-feedback-failed: #d88f7f;
        display: flex; justify-content: flex-end; margin: -1.25rem 0 .25rem;
      }
      .pk-feedback-toggle:hover { background: #1c2126; }
      .pk-feedback-sent { color: #9aa3ac; border-left: 2px solid #4a5a4a; padding-left: .5rem; }
      .pk-feedback-dialog { border-color: #333b44; }

      /* THE PINNED HEADER -- the play page's title, its links and the feedback
         control stay at the top while the story scrolls under them. Sticky,
         not fixed, so it keeps its place in the flow and nothing needs an
         offset to clear it. The page's own background hides the prose passing
         beneath, and the `hr` rule's colour marks the edge. The feedback row's
         negative margin moves onto the header so the resting layout is
         unchanged; `.page-header h1` keeps its default margins. */
      .page-header {
        position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 1; background: #14161a;
        margin-top: -1.25rem; padding-top: .5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid #262b31;
      }
      .page-header .pk-feedback { margin-top: 0; }
      .page-header h1 { margin: .25rem 0 .5rem; }
      .page-header .nav { margin: 0 0 .5rem; }
    </style>
    <main>
      <%# A page that names a header (the play page) gets it pinned to the top
          with the feedback control inside it, so neither scrolls away; any
          other page renders the control on its own, as before. %>
      <% feedback = pocket_kit_feedback(fields: { playthrough_id: (@playthrough.id if @playthrough.is_a?(Playthrough)) }) %>
      <% if content_for?(:header) %>
        <header class="page-header" data-controller="viewport-pin"><%= feedback %><%= yield :header %></header>
      <% else %>
        <%= feedback %>
      <% end %>
      <% if flash[:alert] %><p class="alert"><%= flash[:alert] %></p><% end %>
      <%= yield %>
    </main>
    <span id="ready" hidden></span>
  </body>
</html>
